ner_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"af31e646ae405a3abffe71c3ccb33dd251040bbdc5ab98ec4a5bf3abd0174582","event_uuid":"9b1eca2f-0ece-418b-9e8e-388430e9c118","position":{"commit_sha":"b9d0152b18681d7b9a5f0e30f9a91ef19b4d889f","position":"1787267747615636542-9b1eca2f-0ece-418b-9e8e-388430e9c118"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1787250767449980001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"9f88ffcc-d631-4f2a-8969-22de4b8eb36f","relation":"depends_on","target_id":"054b-790b-d8e8-4aa3"},{"link_uuid":"5d1c03db-57c2-457e-9759-bb562e15f782","relation":"depends_on","target_id":"bd21-f725-0030-47ef"},{"link_uuid":"52f5eeb1-176a-4bc3-b851-82ad4e9e4d80","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"eb1c-3ca4-34f8-4cfb"},{"link_uuid":"e7d21dc9-068e-45a5-90fa-9b31b03ea64c","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"e611-a88c-43c5-45fb"},{"link_uuid":"3dc5caba-1e73-4391-8527-1a3f99153e22","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"8e5a-d6b1-9bf3-4626"},{"link_uuid":"9b1eca2f-0ece-418b-9e8e-388430e9c118","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"45c1-bc2b-70b3-4fbe"}],"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nrebar is a concurrency-heavy project (multi-process writers over a git-backed store, file locks, detached children, CI-level push contention), which makes it both the ideal dogfood target and the worst case for trigger over-fire. Measured over the last 300 non-merge `origin/main` commits by replaying each commit's diff through `content_triggered_overlays` (committed-only vs. with the noisy tier; reproducible — the measurement script is recorded on the session log and in the commit message): the committed high-precision tier fires on 13.3% of diffs (40/300); the noisy tier (`.lock`, `retry`, `Popen`, `fork`, …) adds a further 22.0% (66/300), for 35.3% combined. Three of the four traced corpus bugs (`fine-wisteria-solenodon` via `index.lock`, `fervid-boring-python` via `retry`, `eclectic-spotted-barb` via `Popen`/`start_new_session`) trigger only through noisy-tier vocabulary — so for THIS repo the noisy tier is load-bearing, and per the parent epic's decided design it lives in the project overlay, not the committed list. Nothing currently documents the two-layer trigger design for other projects to copy.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo deliverables. (1) Add a `concurrency` entry to the `code_review` section of this repo's `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` using the project-extension mechanism delivered by the prerequisite story: `trigger_tokens` `[\".lock\", \"retry\", \"Popen\", \"start_new_session\", \"setsid\", \"fork\", \"index.lock\", \"stage_and_commit\", \"push_tickets_branch\", \"refs/reconciler/\"]` and `applies_to` `[\"**/_store/**\", \"**/lock*\", \"**/enrich_drain*\", \".github/workflows/**\"]`. (2) Document the two-layer trigger design in `docs/llm-framework.md` adjacent to the existing code-review routing material (the routing/prompt/eval pointer block around docs/llm-framework.md:661): the committed high-precision tier and its survey grounding, the project noisy tier and how a project adds vocabulary, the measured fire-rate tradeoff (13.3% committed vs +22.0% noisy on this repo, with the reproducible measurement method), and this repo's entry as the worked example. Positive rationale: the dogfood entry is load-bearing (it is what makes the corpus bugs trigger here) and doubles as documentation-by-example, the same way this repo's `project.portability` and `project.engine-layer-enforcement` entries demo the plan-review project overlay.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` — the `code_review.concurrency` project entry.\n- `docs/llm-framework.md` — the two-layer trigger section.\n- `tests/unit/test_code_review_triggers.py` — the config-effect case below (module created by the prerequisite trigger-extension story).\n\n## Testing\n\nConfig-effect contrast (shared test-design standard §6), unit tier: one test proving the project entry changes behavior through the REAL read path — a fixture diff containing `index.lock` (a project token deliberately absent from the committed high-precision list) fires the `concurrency` overlay when `content_triggered_overlays` runs with `repo_root` = this checkout, and does NOT fire under committed-only routing (no `repo_root` / a bare tmp root). A test that only parsed the JSON would miss the read-but-miswired class; this asserts the observable outcome differs across the two routing states.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` carries the `code_review.concurrency` entry with the tokens and globs listed in the Approach; checked by reading the file.\n- [ ] Config-effect test: a diff containing `index.lock` fires `concurrency` under this repo's effective routing and not under committed-only routing; checked by `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_triggers.py`.\n- [ ] `docs/llm-framework.md` documents the two tiers, the fire-rate measurement, and how a project adds vocabulary, using this repo's entry as the worked example; checked by reading the section.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the targeted pytest module pass.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":".rebar/criteria_routing.json","reason":"code_review.concurrency project entry (noisy-tier tokens + globs)"},{"path":"docs/llm-framework.md","reason":"two-layer trigger design documentation"},{"path":"tests/unit/test_code_review_triggers.py","reason":"config-effect contrast test"}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["depends_on","054b-790b-d8e8-4aa3"],["depends_on","bd21-f725-0030-47ef"],["parent","292f-57d1-e554-486f"],["relates_to","45c1-bc2b-70b3-4fbe"],["relates_to","8e5a-d6b1-9bf3-4626"],["relates_to","e611-a88c-43c5-45fb"],["relates_to","eb1c-3ca4-34f8-4cfb"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":"292f-57d1-e554-486f","parent_status_uuid":"3696104e-ea35-499e-8e83-91422033de80","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"in_progress","tags":[],"ticket_id":"5756-ef1a-68ab-4ba4","ticket_type":"story","title":"Dogfood: rebar project trigger vocabulary + two-layer trigger docs","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["3bc79c8d-330a-43b7-90f3-c31c6574597c","98d57616-7894-4866-8e58-cf2201463057","421737db-ee53-45e8-b821-51ce1dc9ba4f","52f5eeb1-176a-4bc3-b851-82ad4e9e4d80","dce700d5-3068-4a51-a655-1822d2aa8d94","9830b399-05ee-405e-ad87-393818951368","3696104e-ea35-499e-8e83-91422033de80","8d003cf7-546c-4be0-97e4-fab599cf5e5a","e7d21dc9-068e-45a5-90fa-9b31b03ea64c","3dc5caba-1e73-4391-8527-1a3f99153e22","9b1eca2f-0ece-418b-9e8e-388430e9c118"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787267747615636543,"uuid":"5307026e-5b05-4cc6-9622-2d137db7accd"}